# Runtime status flags test fixtures (R7)

Clean manifest defaults (R5), the legacy migration (R3), and the GUID
normalizer (R6) can each fail silently and still leave an operator
running production against a test fixture. This is the defense-in-depth
check: at runtime the daemon fingerprints its own resolved configuration
and the configurator shows the count on its main screen.

## The rule

> **Long-running daemons emit a `warnings[]` row in
> `runtime_status.json` whenever any resolved setting matches one of the
> fingerprint patterns below. The row carries `kind` (the pattern that
> matched), `field` (the manifest key name), and `value_fingerprint` (a
> SHA-256 hex digest, NEVER plaintext — see
> [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5).
> Configurator UIs (Win32 and Tauri) read the warnings count and surface
> it on the main screen as a non-dismissible badge until it reaches
> zero.**

The check runs at daemon startup AND on every settings reload. It never
blocks the main loop; warnings are advisory data for the operator.

## Fingerprint patterns

A **resolved** setting value fires a warning if it matches any of:

| Pattern | `kind` |
| --- | --- |
| Hostname suffix `.example` / `.example.com` / `.example.org` / `.example.net` | `placeholder_example_domain` |
| Hostname suffix `.test` / `.invalid` / `.localhost.test` | `placeholder_test_domain` |
| Substring `do-not-ship` (case-insensitive) | `placeholder_do_not_ship` |
| Substring `test-secret` / `test_secret` / `testsecret` | `placeholder_test_secret` |
| Substring `changeme` / `change-me` / `change_me` | `placeholder_changeme` |
| 4+ consecutive `X` in a manifest-declared token / secret | `placeholder_xxxx_token` |
| Exact `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000` | `placeholder_zero_guid` |
| Exact `00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001` | `placeholder_zero_plus_one_guid` |
| Exact `12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012` | `placeholder_sequence_guid` |
| Hex-only (`^[0-9a-fA-F]+$`) under 32 chars in a `kind = "secret"` field | `weak_hex_secret` |
| Substring `localhost` resolved in a `default_prod` context where `prod_must_be_remote = true` | `placeholder_localhost_in_prod` |

The list is a floor; future releases may extend it.

## Required emitted shape

```json
"warnings": [
  {
    "kind": "placeholder_example_domain",
    "field": "SERVER_PUBLIC_URL",
    "value_fingerprint": "0a4d55a8d778e5022fab701977c5d840bbc486d0f9e6b1ddc04b14ff5cef34d5",
    "first_observed_at": "2026-05-19T10:30:00Z"
  }
]
```

`value_fingerprint` is lowercase hex `SHA-256(value_bytes_utf8)`. The
plaintext value is NEVER written to `runtime_status.json`. This matches
the §5 masking contract and lets operators and SIEM correlate "same
value across hosts" without leaking it.

## Required emit-site pattern

```rust
fn fingerprint_warnings(resolved: &ResolvedSettings) -> Vec<Warning> {
    let mut warnings = Vec::new();
    for (key, value) in resolved.iter_all() {
        if let Some(kind) = match_fingerprint(key, value) {
            warnings.push(Warning {
                kind,
                field: key.to_string(),
                value_fingerprint: sha256_hex(value.as_bytes()),
                first_observed_at: OffsetDateTime::now_utc(),
            });
        }
    }
    warnings
}
```

`match_fingerprint` is pure and side-effect-free and must NOT log the
plaintext value even at `trace` level.

## Required configurator-UI surface

Every configurator GUI renders the count on its main screen:

- **Win32:** status line `IDC_LBL_RUNTIME_WARNINGS_COUNT`, e.g.
  `"⚠ 2 placeholder settings detected"`; control hidden when zero.
- **Tauri:** `<div role="status" aria-live="polite">` with the same text.

Both offer a "Details" affordance listing each warning by `field` and
`kind` (never the fingerprint hex — that is for SIEM, not the operator),
with an "Open settings" button that focuses the first offending field.

The count is **non-dismissible**: the operator clears it only by fixing
the setting. WACK and Section 508 both penalize dismissible warnings
that hide critical state.

## Forbidden constructs

- Publishing `runtime_status.json` without calling
  `fingerprint_warnings(&resolved)` first.
- A `warnings[]` row carrying plaintext in any field. Always fingerprint.
- A configurator that suppresses the count ("disable in test mode",
  "hide if dismissed once").
- Logging the resolved configuration map verbatim at startup — mask via
  `pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::*` first.
- Extending the pattern list with shapes that match real customer data
  (e.g. `john@acme.com` as `placeholder_email`). The list is for
  placeholder / test fingerprints only.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation.** `runtime_status.rs` builds its snapshot with
`warnings: vec![]`, never populated. `SERVER_PUBLIC_URL` resolved to
`https://server.example.com:8443` because a manifest default leaked into
prod (R5). The daemon starts happily, the configurator shows zero
warnings, the first sync fails on DNS, and the operator spends 40
minutes chasing a firewall problem that is actually a leaked
placeholder.

**Fix.** Call `fingerprint_warnings(resolved)` before building the
snapshot, log `tracing::warn!(count, fields = ?…)` when non-empty, and
store the rows. The configurator immediately shows `⚠ 1 placeholder
setting detected`; Details names `SERVER_PUBLIC_URL:
placeholder_example_domain`; the badge clears once the value is fixed.

